00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:05.369
SIERRA: Fresh water is one of those things
we forget about when it s easily accessible.

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:11.070
But as soon as it s not there, even for a
short time, it becomes painfully clear how

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:18.090
much we need it. Aside from thirst, we rely
on water to cook, to bathe, to do laundry.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.770
Behind the scenes, much larger quantities
are needed to make our clothes and grow the

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:29.530
food we eat. But as climate change accelerates,
water is becoming scarce in arid, highly-populated

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:36.970
regions all over the world. From India to
Israel, from Italy to Mexico, to Australia.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:47.340
Countries of all kinds are struggling to survive
and adapt to a much drier reality. At the

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:53.240
heart of the problem are outdated rules and
institutions that lack the flexibility demanded

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:58.090
by a changing climate. If new compromises
aren t reached, the problem is going to get

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:07.100
a lot worse. My name is Gabrielle Sierra,
and this is Why It Matters. Today, water scarcity.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:13.140
Al Jazeera News:
It s one thing you can t survive without and

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.550
yet more and more people around the world
are being forced to try.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.200
KHON2 News: Severe drought has hit Maui once
again.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.629
CGTN: China s Taiwan region is facing the
worst water crisis in 56 years.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:29.400
PBS NewsHour: From the Rio Grande to the Rocky
Mountains a mega-drought is underway, it's

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.190
shaping up to be the worst water crisis in
generations.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:39.730
SIERRA: I hear a lot of scary things about
climate change when it comes to this topic,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.840
is the world running out of water?

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.629
POSTEL: The world is not running out of water.
But we may be changing where it is, and how

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:50.670
accessible it is to us.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:58.659
POSTEL: I m Sandra Postel. And I am the director
of the Global Water Policy Project. And what

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:04.360
I try to do is sort of connect the science
and the policy and the practice around water

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.460
so we can have water security for all beings.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.060
SIERRA: Sounds like a big job description.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.030
POSTEL: It's pretty big. I take it in chunks.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.260
SIERRA: The world isn t running out of water.
There is enough to meet the needs of every

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:25.090
human on Earth, or at least there would be
if it was evenly distributed. Water scarcity

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:30.280
isn t about the total amount of water, but
the fact that there isn t enough usable water

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.750
where it is needed. And in these places, climate
change is making the problem worse, fast.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:42.100
POSTEL: The basic physics of it really is
that as the temperature of the planet warms,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:47.780
the atmosphere expands, which means there's
more capacity for the atmosphere to hold water.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:52.379
And so in a dry place, that means you're going
to have more evaporation, which is going to

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:56.950
dry it out further. In a wet place, you're
bringing up more water, and you're going to

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:02.100
get more condensation, and it's going to rain
more intensively. So as a rule of thumb, climate

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:07.950
change will make dry areas drier, wet areas
wetter, and the intensity of those events

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.420
is increasing.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.000
POSTEL: I think the primary way we're going
to experience climate change, and already

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.350
are, is through the water cycle. And we're
already seeing that in the southwestern US,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:25.000
we're in the midst of a mega drought. That
it seems the second worst we've seen in 1200

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.690
years. And it's something we really have to
grapple with. You know, I think we need to

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:35.280
also look at our language around this and
start talking in dry places about aridification

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.610
more than drought, because we're going to
see a long term chronic drying, that requires

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:46.610
a different response than a temporary drought
that we expect to end. There'll be more rain

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.190
in certain years than others, but the long
term trend will be a drying out of the environment,

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:58.030
less water available, less water in the soil.
And that's a different aspect of security

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.060
for society.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.840
SIERRA: Water is a matter of global security
because there are a lot of human beings living

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.130
in those dry environments, whether it be Los
Angeles or Marrakesh. The UN estimates that

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:19.220
water scarcity currently affects 750 million
people globally, and that by 2035, half the

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:25.030
world s population will be living under high
water stress. Alongside the vast human suffering

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.310
it will cause, that much scarcity has the
potential to lead to migration, deep economic

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:36.500
problems, diplomatic faceoffs and even outright
conflict. A big part of the problem is that

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:42.250
the world didn t plan its cities, or its water
infrastructure, with climate change in mind.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:48.420
GIORDANO: A lot of the issues that we face
on water scarcity, as well as water overabundance

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.910
or flooding are relatively recent and what
I mean by relatively recent is the last, you

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.190
know, even 50 years, 75 years.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:01.810
SIERRA: This is Mark Giordano, he holds a
Chair in Environment and International Affairs

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:06.310
at Georgetown University s Walsh School of
Foreign Service.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:10.970
GIORDANO: People didn't talk about water scarcity
in the way they do now or a global water crisis

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.910
until maybe the early 2000s or maybe late,
late 1900s. And I point that out because the

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:20.349
institutions we've developed to manage water
were mostly about building infrastructure

00:06:20.349 --> 00:06:25.060
to access water and to use water, to build
dams, to build canals, to build flood control

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:29.099
systems. Because the questions were usually,
how can we get more water? Now we're asking

00:06:29.099 --> 00:06:32.949
the questions of how can we manage the water
we have better because there is no more water

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:33.949
to keep developing.

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:38.970
GIORDANO: So now we're in a situation where
we've over-allocated the water, our institutions

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.060
were built for something else and now we're
trying to figure out what to do. And a big

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.610
part of it is building understanding that
users of water often don't know there are

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:51.910
other users of water. If you start talking
about the water around wherever you live,

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:57.140
you will very quickly start using, my water,
our water, their water, you ll appropriate

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:01.720
the water yourself. So farmers talk about
their water and cities talk about their water

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.250
and California talks about its water and Colorado
talks about its water or US talks about its

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.040
water versus Mexico s water.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.350
GIORDANO: The number one thing to remember
is that water is a shared resource and it

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:17.780
doesn't belong to any one person, any one
state, any one industry. We re all in it together.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:21.500
We have to make sure we all understand that
and remember that, and that if I forget that,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:28.099
the negative impact on you might come back
to haunt me later. With that kind of basic

00:07:28.099 --> 00:07:34.040
idea there, we have to think about setting
up institutions that are gonna last over time.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:39.270
That means dealing with variability, thinking
through how might the world change and how

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:42.270
can we make sure that we can adapt through
the institutions we built, whether that be

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.680
a formula institution on paper or a social
institution, that's, that's not on paper.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:51.509
SIERRA: In order to understand how climate
change will affect water, first we have to

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:56.340
understand the basics of how water functions
in the environment. It feels like one of those

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.979
101 things that we just never really ask about.
So, let s ask about it.

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:04.810
SIERRA:
So, I grew up in New York and we re actually

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:09.640
kind of famous for our amazing tap water.
You know, people say it s what makes the bagels

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:14.940
and the pizza taste good, but beyond that,
it s also just not something I ve thought

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.660
about a lot in my life. I think people just
kind of expect it to be there. So, can you

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:25.389
tell me just a little bit about how water
works? You know, where does it come from?

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:30.510
POSTEL: You know, most of the water is salt
water, just a very tiny amount, less than

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:35.550
two and a half percent, is freshwater. And
a lot of that two thirds of that is locked

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:41.450
up in glaciers and ice caps. And so we're
dealing with a very small percentage of all

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:46.861
the water on earth that's fresh and drinkable
and useful for all the things we need. So

00:08:46.861 --> 00:08:52.940
having said that, that water cycles, and the
basic supply comes down as rain and snow from

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:59.519
condensation in the atmosphere. And so, it
falls onto the land somewhere. And then depending

00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:04.329
on what's going on, it'll do one of three
things really, it'll run off the land and

00:09:04.329 --> 00:09:09.370
join a creek or a stream and become what we
call runoff. And that'll eventually end up

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:15.640
in the sea, it can soak into the soil, and
then potentially recharge groundwater below

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:22.000
that, or it can evaporate or transpire back
to the atmosphere pretty quickly. So that

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.000
creates the cycle

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:28.050
POSTEL: And then the question is how we use
and manage the water that's in that cycle.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:32.240
And the other thing that's really important,
you grew up in New York, well, every place

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:37.900
has its own water cycle also. Every watershed
has its own cycle, my backyard garden has

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:43.680
a little water cycle. So water cycles on different
timeframes and on different scales. So that

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.350
creates some interesting management challenges
as well.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:52.019
POSTEL: You're going to have a very different
relationship between precipitation and evaporation

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:55.730
if you're in a desert, than if you're in a
tropical rainforest.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:10:02.090
SIERRA: Water is constantly cycling and refreshing
itself according to natural laws. And this

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:08.000
provides a bounty for human use. But if human
beings put too much pressure on the water

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.779
cycle, especially in vulnerable regions, things
can break down. Take groundwater, which collects

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:19.550
under the earth s surface, filling the porous
spaces in soil, rocks, and sediments, contrary

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:25.410
to popular belief it is not actually a giant
pool underground. Anyway, we drill into these

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:30.840
spaces to access and consume this water trapped
inside. Groundwater is then replenished by

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:36.071
rain and snow, as it melts and seeps back
into the land. In places like California s

00:10:36.071 --> 00:10:41.440
central valley, farmers drill deep to pump
out enormous quantities for use and irrigation.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:46.139
In fact, groundwater extraction has been so
aggressive there that the entire region is

00:10:46.139 --> 00:10:51.510
sinking, sometimes at a rate of more than
a foot per year. So the problem is that groundwater

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:58.180
replenishes itself very slowly. It can take
weeks, months, and even years to renew itself.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:01.889
Extract too much too quickly and the source
runs dry.

00:11:01.889 --> 00:11:06.980
POSTEL: We've gotten very good at extracting
water to meet our needs. You know, the way

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:14.910
we primarily do it from rivers, for example,
is to dam them up and create a reservoir,

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:20.720
which can then be delivering water to farmers
and to and to cities, on our own timeframe.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.310
If we're a farmer or a city where there's
groundwater we drill a deep well and then

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:30.910
pump that water up from the ground. And so
we've basically developed these engineering

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:36.200
technologies to access that water and of course,
developed some very big infrastructure to

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:42.300
move the water to where it's needed. You know,
nature has given us kind of a difficult hand

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:46.750
when it comes to water because you need a
lot of sunshine to grow crops. But when you

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:52.070
have a lot of sunshine, you don't have a lot
of rain. And so we've had to move water to

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:56.920
where the crops are best grown. And that often
means, you know, moving water from a wet place

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.270
to a dry place.

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:03.230
SIERRA: What about the relationship between
agriculture and water consumption?

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:09.579
POSTEL: Agriculture is the biggest consumer
of water in the world. About 70% of all the

00:12:09.579 --> 00:12:15.769
water we extract from rivers and groundwater
aquifers around the world goes to agriculture,

00:12:15.769 --> 00:12:20.610
and much of that is consumed, you know, it
doesn't go back into the water cycle right

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:25.840
away. It's transpired by crops back to the
atmosphere, so we can't immediately reuse

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:32.089
it. So agriculture is the primary reason we
see depletion in much of the world. And I

00:12:32.089 --> 00:12:36.490
want to say, you know, a good bit of that
is that it just takes a lot of water to grow

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:42.130
crops. Again, I don't want to castigate agriculture,
farmers are doing great work to provide us

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:48.170
with these items to eat and to make clothes
out of, but it just takes a lot of water.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:53.570
SIERRA:When it comes to agriculture and water
scarcity, the most important question to ask

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:58.370
is where the crops are grown, rather than
how much water they consume. So, that may

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:03.720
sound a bit confusing, but take cocoa for
example. It consumes a lot of water, but it

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.180
s grown in tropical rainforests where there
s lot of rain. California almonds, on the

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:13.829
other hand, consume a lot of water in a very
dry place. From a sustainability perspective,

00:13:13.829 --> 00:13:18.959
that is a problem. Nonetheless, as droughts
have intensified in California over the past

00:13:18.959 --> 00:13:24.450
two decades, almond production has doubled.
Understanding the water cost for any given

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:29.760
product can be hard work. And most of us go
through our daily lives without understanding

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.500
just how big our water footprint is.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:39.230
POSTEL: So if we're an average american, it
takes about 2000 gallons of water to sustain

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:45.529
our daily life, our daily lifestyle. 2000
gallons a day, only five or 10% of that is

00:13:45.529 --> 00:13:50.480
the water we're using at home, and half of
that is in our diet. A third of it is in our

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:57.510
energy use. Maybe 10% is in our computers
and phones and coffee mugs and you know the

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:02.330
things that we use and buy. And then five
or 10% is the water we use at home, indoors

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:03.829
and outdoors. So, it's a lot of water.

00:14:03.829 --> 00:14:06.990
POSTEL: And a lot of it's hiding, you know,
if you look around, everything takes water

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:11.010
to make. And so if we are wearing a cotton
t-shirt we're looking at, you know what, as

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:16.670
much as 700 gallons of water to make that
one cotton shirt. Most of that is water used

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:20.870
to grow the cotton out in the field. You know,
it's a good thing to recognize that one of

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:26.120
the simplest ways in our lives to save water
is to not waste anything.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:31.350
SIERRA: Saving water is something we have
been told to do our whole lives. Whether it

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:35.560
is turning off the tap when you are brushing
your teeth or making sure you take care of

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:40.300
leaks. We re not as used to thinking about
the water that goes into our shirts. But we

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:45.720
have to learn how to think this way, because
increased scarcity is coming. Still, lifestyle

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.620
changes are not enough to address a problem
as large as climate-related scarcity. Only

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:55.900
nations, and groups of nations, have the resources
to build the new, adaptable institutions we

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:00.500
are going to need in our water-scarce future.
After the break, we're going to take a look

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:05.320
at an example that shows exactly what I mean.
Not in the western steppes of China, or the

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.250
drying fields of South Africa, but right here
at home, in the U.S.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:19.380
SIERRA: I wanted to tell you about another
podcast I think you ll like: The Last Archive

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:24.980
from Harvard historian and New Yorker writer
Jill Lepore. This season, Lepore is investigating

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:32.290
purveyors of doubt hoaxers, fraudsters, pseudoscientists
and how the peddling of doubt evolved over

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:39.101
the 20th century, leading all the way to the
Capitol Insurrection on January 6, 2021. This

00:15:39.101 --> 00:15:44.470
show is unlike anything you ve heard before:
history animated by long lost archival tapes

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:50.670
and documents, intrepid reporting, and radio
drama reenactments. The Last Archive unfurls

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:57.319
like a classic 1930s gumshoe mystery but takes
on the big issues of today. How do we know

00:15:57.319 --> 00:16:02.680
what we know, and why does it seem, sometimes,
lately, as if we don t know anything at all?

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:09.190
Listen to The Last Archive wherever you get
your podcasts. From Pushkin Industries.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:19.190
SIERRA: Ok. Example time. Close your eyes.
Imagine you re standing at the top of the

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:26.029
grand canyon on a hot summer day. You hear
the cry of an eagle, and the sound of tourists

00:16:26.029 --> 00:16:31.360
munching sandwiches. Thousands of feet below,
carving its way through the canyon, is the

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:37.560
Colorado river. Fed by the snowmelt of the
Rockies and other Western mountains, the Colorado

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:43.310
is not the largest river in the United States,
but it may be the most important. It is also

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:48.160
the centerpiece of the country s struggle
with water scarcity. Millions of people depend

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:52.740
on it, and every year, it provides less water.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:17:00.420
CBSNews: Red flag fire warnings have been
posted in several western states where a historic

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.160
drought is threatening a catastrophic fire
season.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:07.500
CBS This Morning: Lake Mead, the nation s
largest reservoir, is on track to soon hit

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:09.000
its lowest level ever recorded.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.799
ABC15 Arizona:
For the first time ever, the federal government

00:17:11.799 --> 00:17:16.850
is preparing to declare a drought-emergency
on the lower Colorado River.

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:26.169
POSTEL: It is the lifeline for the American
Southwest, supplies water to about 5 million

00:17:26.169 --> 00:17:34.230
acres of cropland 40 million people get drinking
water from the Colorado, it's shared by two

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:39.910
countries, so it is a binational river, the
US and Mexico. But it hasn't been managed

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:45.360
in a way that really meets the needs of all
of the stakeholders in the basin. And when

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:51.970
the river was divvied up in 1922, you know,
we were coming off of an unusually wet period.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:57.070
And so the thinking was, there's more water
in the river, than it turns out that it naturally

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:00.820
carried even excluding climate issues.

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:07.640
SIERRA: The Colorado River Compact of 1922
was essential for the development of the West.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:11.910
It was built to meet the needs of the time,
to get as much water as possible to the region

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:17.610
s growing cities and farmlands. But it didn
t anticipate climate change or even the notion

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:24.070
of scarcity. The pact guarantees amounts of
water to states, rather than percentages.

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:32.010
So, for example, today the pact entitles California
to 4.4 million acre feet of water per year,

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:38.470
that s well over a trillion gallons. Colorado
gets 3.8 million acre feet, and so on. Millions

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:44.730
of people in these and other states have built
their lives around these amounts. Farms, cities,

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:50.260
sewage systems, hydroelectric plants, every
fountain in Las Vegas, every golf course on

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:56.520
the California coast. Every home for 40 million
people. Meanwhile, climate change means that

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:01.470
there is less snowfall in the Rockies, less
water going into the river, and less water

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:06.500
available to the members of the pact. But
every year, each of those 40 million people

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:08.669
expect the same amount.

00:19:08.669 --> 00:19:14.190
POSTEL: Plus, and this is an important point
going forward, when the water was divvied

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:21.200
up in 1922 there were three important stakeholders
not at the table. One was Mexico, even though

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:26.590
the last 100 miles of the river is in Mexico,
and the delta is in Mexico, and it flows into

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:32.909
the sea in Mexico, Mexico wasn't there. The
Native American tribes that have lived with

00:19:32.909 --> 00:19:38.140
the river in the watershed alongside the river
for generations and generations and generations

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:43.179
and have legally senior rights to the river,
they were not at the table, they have not

00:19:43.179 --> 00:19:49.320
been able to exercise those rights. And third,
the river was not represented at the table.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:51.380
And so the river has had no say.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:57.360
SIERRA: This idea, called environmental personhood
, suggests that natural entities like lakes

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:02.309
and rivers should have the legal right to
resist alteration in a court of law. The idea

00:20:02.309 --> 00:20:07.330
turns Western notions of property rights on
their head, and it's certainly controversial.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:11.730
Still, it has growing momentum around the
world, with indigenous communities leading

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:16.490
the charge. Advocates say it s one of the
best ways to protect threatened waterways

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:21.190
and other natural features. Different versions
of the status have been adopted in countries

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:25.620
like Bangladesh and New Zealand, and even
in the U.S. state of Ohio, along the shores

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:27.000
of Lake Eerie.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:34.020
POSTEL: And so those three omissions immediately
created a problem on top of giving the seven

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:39.250
states in the US more water than the river
usually carries. So it was a problem from

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:44.559
the beginning. And so now we're trying to
go back and fix this. But it's really tough,

00:20:44.559 --> 00:20:48.770
because we're already in a mega drought, there's
way less water than there's been throughout

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:54.890
this history. And yet, we're also trying to
correct the omissions around that table in

00:20:54.890 --> 00:21:00.940
1922. The complication is even more because
you have an upper basin and a lower basin,

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:05.520
and the upper basin, by law of the river is
supposed to provide a set amount of water

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:10.050
to the lower basin, well, that's getting,
you know, more complicated and the lower basin

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:14.909
is where you have more of the demand. That's
where most of the irrigated agriculture is,

00:21:14.909 --> 00:21:20.039
that's where Tucson and Phoenix and Los Angeles
are. And so what's happening is as Lake Mead,

00:21:20.039 --> 00:21:24.270
the biggest reservoir, along with Lake Powell,
they're the two biggest reservoirs in the

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:29.490
country, as those levels go down, they're
approaching a point, and this may happen this

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:37.419
year, where, by the rules of the river right
now, there'll be an enforced reduction in

00:21:37.419 --> 00:21:44.809
allocation of water to Arizona and to Nevada.
If the lake goes below 1075 feet above mean

00:21:44.809 --> 00:21:49.471
sea level, there has to be that reduction.
And I think that may happen this year. And

00:21:49.471 --> 00:21:54.950
so that'll be the first time that these agreed
upon rules kick in, and we'll see what happens.

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:59.860
But, to their credit, the states really have
worked together to develop what they're calling

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:04.090
drought contingency plans. You know, this
drought is long term, we don't know when it's

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:10.420
gonna end. And so Arizona, California, and
Nevada have purposely tried to keep more water

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:15.560
in Lake Mead to avoid getting to that lower
level where, you know, the regulations of

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:20.330
the federal government will kick in. And so
that's cooperation. And that's good. That's

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:24.590
saying we're in this together. But it's still
complicated, and they're already working on

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:30.020
the next round of these rules. So the cooperation
is there, the ability to really solve the

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:33.030
problems long term, you know, we'll see.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:38.260
SIERRA: The pact is still a long way from
comprehensively addressing the West s water

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:43.110
problem, but there is reason for hope. The
agreement has evolved, Mexico gained its missing

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:48.890
seat at the table in 1944, and several smaller
amendments over the years have created stipulations

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:54.409
to improve conservation and sharing during
drought conditions. The capacity for compromise

00:22:54.409 --> 00:22:59.340
has been demonstrated, but there is going
to need to be a lot more before the river

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:01.150
becomes sustainable.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:07.260
SIERRA: What does the Colorado River tell
us about the problems that we're gonna face

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:11.779
and the changes that we're going to have to
get used to as conditions get worse?

00:23:11.779 --> 00:23:18.110
POSTEL: I think the Colorado River reminds
us that that water is life. We depend on rivers

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:24.140
for so many things. You know, we built these
dams on the river for important purposes to

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:30.520
generate electricity to control flooding,
to store water, so we can provide that winter

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:35.720
flood water and snowmelt runoff to farmers
in the summer. So we can provide drinking

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:40.630
water to all of these desert cities that have
sprung up so they're serving very important

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:47.320
purposes. And so the river is really being
asked to do a tremendous amount of work. You're

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:53.919
in a situation of over allocation and increasing
drought and aridification. But we all depend

00:23:53.919 --> 00:23:57.870
on that river and there's just so much it
can give without, you know, kind of dying

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:58.870
out itself.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:03.650
SIERRA: I mean, it sounds like we all need
to get used to a lot more water compromise.

00:24:03.650 --> 00:24:08.990
POSTEL: We do need to get used to more water
compromise and also, you know, we're in a

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:16.640
different moment of time than we were when
we developed the water rights and the laws

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.919
and the engineering infrastructure that went
in decades ago. We're in a different moment

00:24:21.919 --> 00:24:26.000
in time. We have more people, we have more
demand, we have the big concerns about climate

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:31.159
change. We have recurring droughts, we have
recurring floods. So in some ways, you know,

00:24:31.159 --> 00:24:32.919
we need to adapt.

00:24:32.919 --> 00:24:37.720
SIERRA: And the Western United States isn
t alone. The same conflicts are playing out

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:42.900
over rivers, lakes and ground sources across
the world. And in each place you find the

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:48.789
same themes: dwindling supply, overallocation,
and institutions that were not built with

00:24:48.789 --> 00:24:51.020
the flexibility that climate change requires.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:57.390
POSTEL: You know, even though there's a treaty
between India and Pakistan over the Indus

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:03.690
River, we've seen a lot of, you know, conflict
around water related issues in Kashmir, for

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:07.299
example, even though there's a treaty that
sets out how that water should be shared between

00:25:07.299 --> 00:25:12.929
India and Pakistan, we've seen in in Africa,
a lot of tension and violence, even over water

00:25:12.929 --> 00:25:17.909
between different ethnic groups, because there's
not sufficient water there to meet the needs

00:25:17.909 --> 00:25:22.510
of everyone in the in the culture and in the
form of agriculture in particular that they've

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:23.640
been accustomed to.

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:29.080
SIERRA: One of the most talked-about cases
of water scarcity is Capetown, South Africa,

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:35.220
which in 2017 came within a hair s breadth
of becoming the first major city to run out

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:41.409
of water completely. Preparing for the worst,
Capetown s day zero plans included shutting

00:25:41.409 --> 00:25:47.390
off pipes, and deploying resources to deal
with social unrest and outbreaks of disease.

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:53.420
Strong rains staved off the emergency in 2018,
but water anxiety remains a serious problem

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:57.520
in South Africa and in many other parts of
the Continent too.

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:04.110
POSTEL: And so, again, it takes many forms.
And for example, the Nile basin, you know,

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:11.440
84% of the water is coming from Ethiopia.
But Egypt is, from a geopolitical and military

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:16.899
sense, the most powerful. And so that's where
you could run into trouble, because the upstream

00:26:16.899 --> 00:26:22.350
country has, in a sense, the hydrologic power,
but not the military power. You look at all

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:26.850
the rivers that come out of China, well, China
has the military power and the hydrologic

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:31.250
power, so you're not necessarily going to
see a war there. But the downstream countries

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:37.220
are potentially at risk of water shortage
of not having the security of water that they

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:41.900
need because of decisions made upstream. And
so you have to look at the dynamic from both

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:45.320
the geopolitical sense and from the hydrologic
side.

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:51.610
SIERRA: Water scarcity is local, but it doesn
t always stay local. In a world where we import

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:56.309
so many things, water problems in one place
can easily trickle over to another.

00:26:56.309 --> 00:27:01.260
GIORDAN: When people talk about water and
conflict or water and politics, they're often

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:06.720
thinking about the direct issues or the direct
outcomes in some place. But there's so much

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:12.039
trade in agriculture now and many countries
are very dependent on agricultural imports

00:27:12.039 --> 00:27:18.050
that a water problem in one place can transfer
to another place via the trade. If people

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:22.760
cannot afford food, they are not going to
be happy and you can expect food price rises

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:26.909
to be associated with political instability,
particularly in places where people spend

00:27:26.909 --> 00:27:32.360
a lot of their income on food. And so, that
s a mechanism where a drought in one country

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:33.990
can affect another.

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:39.450
SIERRA: Cross-border water inequalities can
lead to tension. For decades, movies like

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:44.850
Mad Max have portrayed water wars as the endgame
of environmental degradation.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:57.457
MadMax: Fury Road: Do not my friends, become
addicted to water, it will take hold of you

00:27:57.457 --> 00:27:58.457
and you will resent its absence

00:27:58.457 --> 00:28:02.810
SIERRA: Do you think that there's potential
for conflict over water, as you know, scarcity

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:04.950
and overconsumption continue?

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:10.230
POSTEL: There definitely is potential for
conflict over access to water. We've already

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:16.110
seen this historically, we can see conflict
between countries, we can see conflict within

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:20.880
countries, we can see conflict between cities
and farms, we can see conflict between different

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:26.899
ethnic groups. And we have seen this and I
think we do need to be aware that this could

00:28:26.899 --> 00:28:33.169
worsen over time. And I particularly worry
about the effect of food prices and rising

00:28:33.169 --> 00:28:39.450
food prices in particular on that social and
political stability. We need to really be

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:46.570
on the alert for these kinds of social and
political events and really work to prevent

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:49.679
that rise in civil unrest from getting out
of hand.

00:28:49.679 --> 00:28:54.380
SIERRA: But not everyone believes water conflict
is inevitable, or even the most important

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:55.590
part of the story.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:29:01.919
GIORDANO: The premise of the water war concept
is water supplies are fixed, that global populations

00:29:01.919 --> 00:29:07.330
are rising and in addition to that, as populations
are getting wealthier, they're consuming more

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:12.630
meat and, if you're gonna grow beef or pork
or chicken, you know, you have to feed multiple

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:17.940
kilos of that grain to that meat. So meat
consumption per capita is rising and the number

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:21.600
of people is rising and the water is fixed
and so people extrapolated that to say, well,

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:25.080
at some point, because water is so precious,
countries are going to fight over it.

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:29.779
GIORDANO: But the real story of water at the
international level has been one of cooperation,

00:29:29.779 --> 00:29:34.570
not one of outright conflict. As you go down
in scale, that's where you're more likely

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:39.230
to find actual conflict and in particular
violent conflict and you can find hundreds

00:29:39.230 --> 00:29:43.740
of examples of two farmers shot each other.
You're gonna be hard pressed to find an example

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:48.210
of of two countries. So, what I would say
connects also to the trade issue we talked

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:53.230
about, within countries, poor water management
coupled with a drought, coupled with other

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:58.511
pressures can lead to conflicts, can be a
factor in conflicts within a state. Sometimes

00:29:58.511 --> 00:30:02.780
those conflicts can spill over. People discuss
that, especially with Middle Eastern states

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:05.660
and some Sahalian states in Africa now.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:11.890
SIERRA: Conflict over water, whether between
two farmers, two cities, or even, in the worst-case

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:17.940
scenario, two nations, becomes likelier as
supply dries up. But there are solutions out

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:23.860
there. There is enough water to meet the needs
of a growing global population, but there

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:28.960
is not enough water to continue all of the
habits we developed before climate change.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:34.399
In order to create sustainability, we are
going to have to compromise, as individuals

00:30:34.399 --> 00:30:39.620
and as societies. And we are going to have
to build institutions that have compromise

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.050
and flexibility at their core.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:48.590
POSTEL: With better policies and incentives,
I think we can have, you know, happy, healthy,

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:53.659
productive lives, while consuming less water.
You know, I don't need to eat red meat every

00:30:53.659 --> 00:30:57.690
day. If we look at our diets, you know, the
amount of water that goes into producing the

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:04.100
hamburger is way more than goes into producing
a delicious non meat meal. And so even if

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:09.260
we reduce our meat consumption by half, say
that's going to save water, you're not using

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:15.200
all of that irrigation water to grow alfalfa
to feed to the cows, or to grow corn to feed

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:21.169
to the cows. And so we have a lot of ability
to adjust. I think we need to do it in an

00:31:21.169 --> 00:31:25.700
equitable way, I think we need to do it in
a way that gives people important choice.

00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:29.860
You know, diet is a very personal thing, I
don't think we should be prescriptive about

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:34.200
it, but to provide the information and let
people know, hey, you know, some of this is

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:39.630
in our control. Some of this is in our hands.
And we've also only begun to tap the potential

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:46.080
of conservation and increased efficiency of
water use. We're saving because of more efficient

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:52.370
toilets and faucets and showerheads, and dishwashers
and washing machines, were saving 9 billion

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:57.210
gallons of water a day, that's equivalent
to nine New York City's worth of water because

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:02.380
of more efficient appliances and fixtures
in our homes. That's really huge. And we haven't

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:08.570
begun to tap the potential in irrigated agriculture,
more efficient delivery of water, more efficient

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:14.700
application of water, the ability to put sensors
in the soil to know how much water is really

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:20.840
needed to grow the crops, we can marry information
technology to efficiency technology and get

00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:26.310
more crop per drop across the board. So there's
a lot we can do to solve these problems, it's

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:32.269
not a doomsday scenario, we just need to develop
the political will, the social will to make

00:32:32.269 --> 00:32:38.510
these changes and they can be made. My concern
is that the problems are unfolding, the challenges

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:44.320
are unfolding much faster than these solutions.
Everywhere I look around the world where there

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:50.320
is a problem related to breaking the water
cycle, there is an example of how to fix it

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:53.790
in the real world. We just need to scale these
solutions up.

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:59.760
GIORDANO: A big part is developing the understanding
that there are other people using this water

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:04.899
and so communication and awareness is a big
part of the institution building process.

00:33:04.899 --> 00:33:11.309
And I would say on the negative side, we haven't
done that very well so far. On the positive

00:33:11.309 --> 00:33:15.889
side, institutions take a long time to build
and trust takes a long time to build, and

00:33:15.889 --> 00:33:20.269
we're moving in a path in the right direction,
but these processes take time. So I always

00:33:20.269 --> 00:33:24.860
say, short-term, I'm a pessimist and, and
long-term, I'm an optimist.

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:28.929
GIORDANO: I think the only thing I'd say is
water, just to get everybody to step back

00:33:28.929 --> 00:33:33.360
and think about how they feel about water.
And when I say feel, I actually mean really

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:39.419
feel, like, you know, there's a reason that
you want the hotel room with the sea view

00:33:39.419 --> 00:33:43.890
and there's a reason people wanna sit by the
river. There's something about water that

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:49.120
is special to us. You'll be hard placed to
find any religion that doesn't have water

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:54.929
as part of its origin story of, of humankind
or, or the universe. It has a special place

00:33:54.929 --> 00:33:59.529
for us. It has a special impact on us in a
way that few other things do. And I think

00:33:59.529 --> 00:34:04.650
if we stop and think about that for a while,
how important it is to all of us in every

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:09.730
aspect of our life, that maybe we can think
a little bit more clearly about coming up

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:11.050
with good solutions.

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:18.419
SIERRA: Can you paint me a picture, what's
at stake, if we don't learn to cooperate and

00:34:18.419 --> 00:34:19.850
conserve better?

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:27.149
POSTEL: We're looking at a world that will
be less good for us to live in. We will not

00:34:27.149 --> 00:34:34.899
have the healthy rivers and riverside forests
and fish and wildlife that we want to have

00:34:34.899 --> 00:34:39.630
in our world, they will be gone. And then
secondly, you know, we'll see more conflict

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:45.339
because water is life, water is essential
to food, water is essential for drinking.

00:34:45.339 --> 00:34:50.660
And as we see, as things become scarce, they
also grow more inequitable, so of course,

00:34:50.660 --> 00:34:54.880
the wealthy are going to get what they need,
and the poor will not get what they need.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:35:00.480
And so there'll be more inequity, which breeds
more tension, which breeds more violence.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:05.700
And so we'll have a less peaceful world as
well as a less healthy world. And that's not

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:07.840
a world that most of us want to live in.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:27.540
